The driver only declares a 50 us transition latency, from which the cpufreq core derives schedutil's rate limit:
75 us. Every frequency change is a mailbox request to MP1, so at idle the governor makes about 275 requests a
second, its kthread (sugov:0) is the busiest task, and the cores sit at 2.3 to 2.6 GHz with nothing running.

Set transition_delay_us to 10 ms, the value the core caps slow drivers at. Found and measured by fitamix
(https://github.com/fitamix/ps5-bazzite-toolbox#cpufreq), 20 s at idle in Game Mode each:

    rate_limit_us          75 (default)   10000
    frequency changes/s    ~275           ~91
    sugov:0 CPU time       ~20 % core     ~9 %
    average core clock     ~2.4 GHz       ~800 MHz

Games still reach the full clock.
---
diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/ps5-cpufreq.c b/drivers/cpufreq/ps5-cpufreq.c
index cfe6d09fd..c442e2a7b 100644
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/ps5-cpufreq.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/ps5-cpufreq.c
@@ -75,6 +75,11 @@ static int ps5_cpufreq_cpu_init(struct cpufreq_policy *policy)
 	cpumask_copy(policy->cpus, cpu_present_mask);
 	policy->freq_table = ps5_freq_table;
 	policy->cpuinfo.transition_latency = PS5_TRANSITION_LATENCY_NS;
+	/*
+	 * Every change is a mailbox request to MP1. From the latency alone schedutil would re-evaluate every
+	 * 75 us: hundreds of requests a second at idle, its kthread the busiest task, the cores never at the floor.
+	 */
+	policy->transition_delay_us = 10 * USEC_PER_MSEC;
 	policy->boost_supported = true;
 	policy->cur = PS5_CPU_MHZ_FLOOR * 1000;
 
